WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Welcome to Coordination Compounds.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.560
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.740
Our first habit is to read the brackets before counting anything.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.900
A coordination entity contains a central atom or ion and its directly bonded

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:16.300
ligands.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.920
A ligand donates an electron pair through a donor atom.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.960
Species outside the brackets are counterions, not automatically ligands.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.840
Consider the cobalt complex on the board.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.058
Three chloride counterions require the bracketed entity to have charge

00:00:32.058 --> 00:00:32.540
positive

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:32.900
three.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.660
The abbreviation e n stands for ethane one two diamine.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.500
Each of these ligands is neutral, so cobalt has oxidation state plus three.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.060
Now count donor atoms separately.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:48.680
Each e n attaches through two nitrogen atoms.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.800
Three such bidentate ligands give six directly attached donors.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:57.820
The coordination number is six, not three.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.780
Oxidation state, complex charge, ligand count and donor count are four

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.540
different quantities.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.880
Werner's primary valency is associated with oxidation state, while secondary

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.360
valency is associated with coordination number and directed bonding.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.600
Water and ammonia are normally monodentate.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.120
A chelating ligand attaches through several donor atoms and forms a ring with

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:18.480
the metal.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.700
E D T A can bind through six donor atoms in a common hexadentate arrangement.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.300
An ambidentate ligand is different: it can use alternative donor atoms.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.908
Nitrite can bind through nitrogen or oxygen, but that does not mean it

00:01:32.908 --> 00:01:33.300
normally

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.880
binds through both simultaneously.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.132
For a second charge check, take hexacyanidoferrate with overall charge

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:40.600
negative

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:40.880
four.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.000
Six cyanide ligands contribute negative six.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.500
The iron oxidation state must be plus two to give negative four overall.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.448
Write that short charge equation before assigning any electronic

00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:53.080
configuration.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.757
The ligand's charge belongs in the charge balance; its denticity belongs in

00:01:57.757 --> 00:01:57.920
the

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:58.360
donor count.

00:01:58.752 --> 00:02:01.392
Naming becomes easier after charge balance.

00:02:02.072 --> 00:02:06.432
Name ligands alphabetically, ignoring multiplicative prefixes, then name the

00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:08.672
metal with its oxidation state in Roman numerals.

00:02:09.392 --> 00:02:13.312
Ammonia is called ammine, with two m letters, and water is aqua.

00:02:13.692 --> 00:02:18.552
An anionic complex uses a metal name ending in ate; iron uses ferrate.

00:02:19.232 --> 00:02:23.572
Thus the potassium salt of the negative four hexacyanidoferrate ion is

00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:26.192
potassium hexacyanidoferrate, two.

00:02:26.192 --> 00:02:30.632
The Roman numeral refers to iron's oxidation state, not the ion's charge.

00:02:31.312 --> 00:02:35.212
Now consider the cobalt salt with four ammonia ligands, two chlorido ligands

00:02:35.212 --> 00:02:37.712
inside the brackets and one chloride outside.

00:02:38.312 --> 00:02:40.252
The complex has charge positive one.

00:02:40.932 --> 00:02:44.532
Four neutral ammonia ligands contribute zero and the two bound chlorides

00:02:44.532 --> 00:02:45.712
contribute negative two.

00:02:46.392 --> 00:02:48.012
Cobalt is therefore plus three.

00:02:48.012 --> 00:02:50.652
Ammine precedes chlorido alphabetically.

00:02:50.932 --> 00:02:54.881
The name is tetraammine dichlorido cobalt, three, chloride, as written

00:02:54.881 --> 00:02:55.332
together

00:02:55.332 --> 00:02:55.852
on the board.

00:02:56.472 --> 00:03:01.072
Its octahedral geometry allows cis and trans forms; the formula alone does not

00:03:01.072 --> 00:03:02.212
tell us which one we have.

00:03:03.052 --> 00:03:05.992
Next, distinguish bound chloride from ionizable chloride.

00:03:06.792 --> 00:03:10.832
Our worked salt has five ammonia ligands and one chloride inside the brackets,

00:03:11.212 --> 00:03:12.412
with two chlorides outside.

00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:16.952
We assume an immediate silver nitrate test that leaves the coordination sphere

00:03:16.952 --> 00:03:17.392
intact.

00:03:18.052 --> 00:03:21.592
Under that assumption only the two outer chlorides form silver chloride.

00:03:21.592 --> 00:03:26.712
A sample containing zero point zero one zero zero mole of salt therefore gives

00:03:26.712 --> 00:03:29.592
zero point zero two zero zero mole of silver chloride.

00:03:30.572 --> 00:03:34.372
Multiply by one hundred forty three point five grams per mole to obtain two

00:03:34.372 --> 00:03:35.412
point eight seven grams.

00:03:36.252 --> 00:03:39.252
Cobalt is still plus three and its coordination number is six.

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:43.792
Counting all three chlorines as precipitated chloride would ignore the test's

00:03:43.792 --> 00:03:44.452
stated assumption.

00:03:45.412 --> 00:03:49.112
Bound ligands are not permanently impossible to displace; reaction conditions

00:03:49.112 --> 00:03:49.952
and time matter.

00:03:50.532 --> 00:03:54.332
In this question, the intact sphere assumption tells us exactly what to count.

00:03:54.552 --> 00:03:58.852
Coordination number is useful, but it does not uniquely fix geometry.

00:03:59.792 --> 00:04:01.952
Six donors commonly give an octahedral environment.

00:04:02.952 --> 00:04:05.552
Four can give tetrahedral or square planar geometry.

00:04:06.472 --> 00:04:09.952
In elementary valence bond language these are described using orbital

00:04:09.952 --> 00:04:14.667
combinations such as s p three for tetrahedral and d s p two for square

00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:15.132
planar.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:19.612
These labels are a model, not a complete explanation of spectra.

00:04:20.352 --> 00:04:21.972
Isomerism needs a separate check.

00:04:22.772 --> 00:04:27.132
Structural isomers differ in connectivity or in which species lie inside the

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:27.892
coordination sphere.

00:04:28.812 --> 00:04:32.032
Linkage isomers use different donor atoms of an ambidentate ligand.

00:04:32.892 --> 00:04:36.172
Ionization isomers exchange a coordinated ion with a counterion.

00:04:36.932 --> 00:04:39.972
A drawing merely rotated on the page is not a new isomer.

00:04:40.812 --> 00:04:44.912
For square planar platinum with two ammonia and two chloride ligands, adjacent

00:04:44.912 --> 00:04:48.632
chlorides define the cis form, while opposite chlorides define trans.

00:04:49.072 --> 00:04:53.012
The angle between the two platinum to chloride bond directions is ninety

00:04:53.012 --> 00:04:55.872
degrees in cis and one hundred eighty in trans.

00:04:56.732 --> 00:04:59.532
These are spatial arrangements with the same connectivity.

00:05:00.472 --> 00:05:05.052
An octahedral complex with three monodentate A ligands and three monodentate B

00:05:05.052 --> 00:05:07.692
ligands can have facial and meridional forms.

00:05:08.432 --> 00:05:12.132
In the facial form, the three A donors occupy a triangular face.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:15.392
Every A to metal to A angle is ninety degrees.

00:05:15.892 --> 00:05:20.085
In the meridional form, one pair of A ligands is opposite, giving a one

00:05:20.085 --> 00:05:20.499
hundred

00:05:20.499 --> 00:05:21.352
eighty degree angle.

00:05:22.372 --> 00:05:25.072
Draw or label the six positions before counting arrangements.

00:05:26.072 --> 00:05:28.712
Optical isomers are non superimposable mirror images.

00:05:29.532 --> 00:05:33.312
The cobalt complex with three bidentate e n ligands can form such a pair.

00:05:34.172 --> 00:05:38.472
Equal amounts of both mirror forms make a racemic mixture with no net optical

00:05:38.472 --> 00:05:38.952
rotation.

00:05:39.652 --> 00:05:42.032
That does not make the individual structures identical.

00:05:42.792 --> 00:05:46.392
Detailed three dimensional optical drawings remain further practice.

00:05:47.392 --> 00:05:51.592
First establish connectivity and geometry, then ask which particular kind of

00:05:51.592 --> 00:05:53.032
isomerism is possible.

00:05:53.856 --> 00:05:57.816
Crystal field theory separates the five d orbitals into groups with different

00:05:57.816 --> 00:05:58.236
energies.

00:05:58.876 --> 00:06:03.376
In an octahedral field, the three t two g orbitals are lower and the two e g

00:06:03.376 --> 00:06:04.416
orbitals are higher.

00:06:05.496 --> 00:06:09.456
Relative to the barycentre, a t two g electron contributes minus zero point

00:06:09.456 --> 00:06:14.916
four delta octahedral, and an e g electron contributes plus zero point six

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:15.756
delta octahedral.

00:06:16.396 --> 00:06:19.036
Those contributions alone exclude pairing energy.

00:06:19.836 --> 00:06:21.176
Consider a d six metal ion.

00:06:21.526 --> 00:06:24.956
If splitting is small compared with the cost of pairing, the high spin

00:06:24.956 --> 00:06:29.016
arrangement has four electrons in t two g and two in e g.

00:06:29.016 --> 00:06:32.596
Its orbital occupancy contains four unpaired electrons and one pair.

00:06:33.256 --> 00:06:37.096
If splitting is sufficiently large, the low spin arrangement puts all six

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:39.316
electrons into the lower set as three pairs.

00:06:39.836 --> 00:06:41.596
There are then no unpaired electrons.

00:06:42.536 --> 00:06:44.776
Let's calculate the high spin field contribution.

00:06:44.776 --> 00:06:49.976
Four times minus zero point four, plus two times zero point six, gives minus

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:51.716
zero point four delta octahedral.

00:06:52.536 --> 00:06:56.636
The low spin field contribution is six times minus zero point four, or minus

00:06:56.636 --> 00:06:58.276
two point four delta octahedral.

00:06:59.156 --> 00:07:01.436
Now include P as the cost per electron pair.

00:07:02.236 --> 00:07:05.236
High spin has energy minus zero point four delta plus P.

00:07:05.816 --> 00:07:09.796
Low spin has minus two point four delta plus three P.

00:07:09.796 --> 00:07:12.736
Comparing only the negative field terms would miss the extra pairing cost.

00:07:13.436 --> 00:07:18.109
With delta equal to two hundred fifty kilojoules per mole and P equal to one

00:07:18.109 --> 00:07:22.116
hundred eighty, high spin gives positive eighty and low spin negative sixty

00:07:22.116 --> 00:07:24.916
kilojoules per mole on the same chosen energy reference.

00:07:25.776 --> 00:07:29.536
Low spin minus high spin is negative one hundred forty.

00:07:29.536 --> 00:07:33.236
Low spin is lower by one hundred forty kilojoules per mole in this simplified

00:07:33.236 --> 00:07:33.496
model.

00:07:34.276 --> 00:07:37.716
The crossover is when the octahedral splitting equals the pairing energy.

00:07:37.716 --> 00:07:42.880
Do not transfer this octahedral orbital diagram unchanged to a square planar

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:43.016
or

00:07:43.016 --> 00:07:44.016
tetrahedral complex.

00:07:44.304 --> 00:07:46.984
Unpaired electrons produce paramagnetism.

00:07:46.984 --> 00:07:49.804
An all paired arrangement is diamagnetic.

00:07:50.424 --> 00:07:55.338
The spin only magnetic moment is the square root of n times n plus two, in

00:07:55.338 --> 00:07:55.604
Bohr

00:07:55.604 --> 00:07:58.784
magnetons, where n is the number of unpaired electrons.

00:07:59.464 --> 00:08:02.584
This is an approximation; orbital contributions can matter.

00:08:03.424 --> 00:08:07.584
Our high spin octahedral d six example has four unpaired electrons.

00:08:08.324 --> 00:08:12.334
The square root of four times six is about four point nine zero Bohr

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:12.924
magnetons.

00:08:14.024 --> 00:08:16.304
Low spin d six has no unpaired electrons.

00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:19.264
Now compare the two nickel complexes on the board.

00:08:20.104 --> 00:08:24.764
Each has four singly negative ligands and total charge negative two, so both

00:08:24.764 --> 00:08:27.244
contain nickel in oxidation state plus two.

00:08:28.064 --> 00:08:31.924
Nickel two has a d eight configuration, after removal of the two four s

00:08:31.924 --> 00:08:33.284
electrons from neutral nickel.

00:08:33.964 --> 00:08:37.784
The chloride complex is usually tetrahedral with two unpaired electrons.

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:42.464
Its spin only moment is the square root of eight, about two point eight three

00:08:42.464 --> 00:08:43.384
Bohr magnetons.

00:08:44.224 --> 00:08:48.864
The cyanide complex is square planar and all paired, so it is diamagnetic.

00:08:49.704 --> 00:08:53.824
The same metal, oxidation state and coordination number did not guarantee the

00:08:53.824 --> 00:08:55.704
same geometry or magnetism.

00:08:55.704 --> 00:08:57.544
Colour needs another check.

00:08:58.444 --> 00:09:02.324
Absorption between split d levels can remove visible wavelengths, leaving a

00:09:02.324 --> 00:09:05.664
complementary transmitted or reflected colour.

00:09:05.664 --> 00:09:08.324
Ligands, oxidation state and geometry affect the splitting.

00:09:09.304 --> 00:09:13.764
Charge transfer transitions can also produce colour, so d zero or d ten does

00:09:13.764 --> 00:09:16.864
not guarantee that every compound is colourless.

00:09:16.864 --> 00:09:21.084
Complex formation is useful in qualitative analysis, including the deep blue

00:09:21.084 --> 00:09:25.524
copper ammine species in aqueous ammonia, and in biological metal binding,

00:09:25.524 --> 00:09:25.764
such

00:09:25.764 --> 00:09:28.784
as iron in haem proteins and magnesium in chlorophyll.

00:09:29.424 --> 00:09:34.404
Finish with the sequence: complex charge, oxidation state, donor count, d

00:09:34.404 --> 00:09:36.824
electron count, geometry, then properties.

00:09:37.704 --> 00:09:41.377
Use the twelve original practice questions and the seven worked examples in

00:09:41.377 --> 00:09:41.524
the

00:09:41.524 --> 00:09:42.004
written companion.

00:09:42.824 --> 00:09:46.844
This is a recorded Unit twelve foundation, not exhaustive coverage or official

00:09:46.844 --> 00:09:47.844
past paper material.

00:09:48.216 --> 00:09:53.775
Isomerism can change which ion is outside the coordination sphere or which

00:09:53.775 --> 00:09:54.076
atom

00:09:54.076 --> 00:09:54.896
binds to the metal.

00:09:55.856 --> 00:10:00.216
Compare a cobalt complex with five neutral ammonia ligands and coordinated

00:10:00.216 --> 00:10:04.896
bromide, accompanied by a sulfate counterion, with one containing coordinated

00:10:04.896 --> 00:10:07.076
sulfate and an external bromide.

00:10:07.976 --> 00:10:09.276
Cobalt is plus three in each.

00:10:09.816 --> 00:10:14.736
In the first case, the complex charge is plus two; in the second, it is plus

00:10:14.736 --> 00:10:15.076
one.

00:10:15.356 --> 00:10:20.236
The overall elemental inventory is unchanged, but the counterions differ.

00:10:21.316 --> 00:10:25.267
This illustrates ionization isomerism, assuming the stated coordination

00:10:25.267 --> 00:10:25.656
spheres

00:10:25.656 --> 00:10:27.596
persist under the test conditions.

00:10:28.796 --> 00:10:30.496
Linkage isomerism is different.

00:10:31.396 --> 00:10:35.136
An ambidentate nitrite ligand can attach through nitrogen or through oxygen,

00:10:35.816 --> 00:10:39.716
giving different metal to donor connectivity without exchanging the external

00:10:39.716 --> 00:10:40.396
counterion.

00:10:41.156 --> 00:10:45.496
Ambidentate does not mean both donor atoms necessarily bind simultaneously to

00:10:45.496 --> 00:10:45.916
this metal.

00:10:46.876 --> 00:10:50.716
A bidentate ligand instead supplies two donor attachments in the specified

00:10:50.716 --> 00:10:51.296
complex.

00:10:52.576 --> 00:10:55.476
Classify the structural change before applying an isomer name.

00:10:56.876 --> 00:11:00.956
Formula brackets identify the coordination sphere, so moving a group across a

00:11:00.956 --> 00:11:04.716
bracket changes chemical information rather than merely changing typography.

00:11:05.184 --> 00:11:10.004
For an octahedral complex with three identical A ligands and three identical B

00:11:10.004 --> 00:11:14.384
ligands, facial and meridional arrangements are distinct geometrical isomers.

00:11:15.264 --> 00:11:19.404
Imagine six ligand positions on the positive and negative x, y and z axes.

00:11:20.364 --> 00:11:24.466
Opposite ends of the same axis are trans, while positions on different axes

00:11:24.466 --> 00:11:24.904
are cis.

00:11:25.564 --> 00:11:30.304
A facial arrangement places the three A ligands at positive x, positive y and

00:11:30.304 --> 00:11:30.824
positive z.

00:11:31.384 --> 00:11:35.744
Every A pair is cis, and the three positions form one face of the octahedron.

00:11:36.424 --> 00:11:41.064
A meridional arrangement can place A at positive x, negative x and positive z.

00:11:41.664 --> 00:11:45.204
The first two form a trans pair, while the other two A pairs are cis.

00:11:45.924 --> 00:11:49.358
The schematic shows these position labels so the classification does not

00:11:49.358 --> 00:11:49.644
depend

00:11:49.644 --> 00:11:51.164
on an ambiguous flat sketch.

00:11:52.364 --> 00:11:55.164
Rotating an entire complex does not create a new isomer.

00:11:56.224 --> 00:11:57.364
Exchanging ligand positions can.

00:11:57.924 --> 00:12:02.144
These fac and mer labels apply to this octahedral three plus three pattern; do

00:12:02.144 --> 00:12:05.784
not reuse them for every six coordinate formula without checking its ligand

00:12:05.784 --> 00:12:06.124
arrangement.

00:12:06.432 --> 00:12:11.168
A chelating ligand binds through multiple donor atoms to the same central

00:12:11.168 --> 00:12:11.492
metal

00:12:11.492 --> 00:12:12.952
and closes a ring.

00:12:14.052 --> 00:12:19.732
Ethane one two diamine, commonly abbreviated e n, can bind through its two

00:12:19.732 --> 00:12:20.532
nitrogen atoms.

00:12:21.592 --> 00:12:25.872
Three such ligands around cobalt give six donor attachments, so the

00:12:25.872 --> 00:12:29.792
coordination number is six although there are only three ligand molecules.

00:12:30.482 --> 00:12:34.872
With neutral diamine ligands and complex charge plus three, cobalt has

00:12:34.872 --> 00:12:36.632
oxidation state plus three.

00:12:37.492 --> 00:12:42.072
An octahedral tris chelate arrangement can have two nonsuperimposable mirror

00:12:42.072 --> 00:12:46.552
image forms, conventionally described by delta and lambda helicity labels.

00:12:47.512 --> 00:12:50.572
They have the same connectivity but a different three dimensional handed

00:12:50.572 --> 00:12:51.012
arrangement.

00:12:52.072 --> 00:12:56.332
Mirror images that can be superimposed do not constitute an optical isomer

00:12:56.332 --> 00:12:56.652
pair.

00:12:57.182 --> 00:13:02.492
A flat donor count table alone cannot establish handedness; a spatial model is

00:13:02.492 --> 00:13:03.352
needed for that assignment.

00:13:04.452 --> 00:13:08.366
Individual enantiomers can rotate plane polarized light, while an equal

00:13:08.366 --> 00:13:08.752
mixture

00:13:08.752 --> 00:13:11.572
has cancelling rotations under the same conditions.

00:13:12.712 --> 00:13:16.553
Delta and lambda specify structural helicity; they do not automatically

00:13:16.553 --> 00:13:16.932
predict

00:13:16.932 --> 00:13:18.872
the sign of measured optical rotation.

00:13:18.872 --> 00:13:24.752
Keep ligand count, donor count, oxidation state and chirality as separate

00:13:24.752 --> 00:13:25.172
questions.

00:13:25.584 --> 00:13:29.364
Complex stability is an equilibrium question that needs a defined reaction.

00:13:30.444 --> 00:13:36.084
Suppose a metal M binds one ligand L to make M L, then binds another to make M

00:13:36.084 --> 00:13:37.204
L two.

00:13:37.204 --> 00:13:40.584
Define the first and second stepwise formation constants from those two

00:13:40.584 --> 00:13:41.064
equilibria.

00:13:42.144 --> 00:13:45.504
Adding the reactions gives metal plus two ligands forming the bis ligand

00:13:45.504 --> 00:13:46.044
complex.

00:13:47.044 --> 00:13:51.104
Multiplying the stepwise constants gives the overall formation constant beta

00:13:51.104 --> 00:13:51.824
two.

00:13:51.824 --> 00:13:55.129
With illustrative dimensionless constants ten to the four and ten to the

00:13:55.129 --> 00:13:55.404
three,

00:13:55.404 --> 00:13:57.344
beta two is ten to the seven.

00:13:57.964 --> 00:14:01.804
It is not their sum, and the second constant need not always exceed the first.

00:14:02.664 --> 00:14:06.110
Thermodynamic constants use activities; concentration ratios are

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:06.864
approximations

00:14:06.864 --> 00:14:08.604
under specified solution conditions.

00:14:09.344 --> 00:14:13.564
A large overall formation constant favours the complex relative to free metal

00:14:13.564 --> 00:14:18.224
and free ligand for that defined equilibrium, but the actual composition also

00:14:18.224 --> 00:14:22.144
depends on ligand availability, competing reactions and mass balances.

00:14:23.364 --> 00:14:26.104
Thermodynamic stability is distinct from kinetic inertness.

00:14:26.884 --> 00:14:30.544
A complex can be strongly favoured at equilibrium while exchanging ligands

00:14:30.544 --> 00:14:34.704
rapidly, or react slowly even when a different product is favoured.

00:14:34.704 --> 00:14:38.864
Coordination compounds connect reaction chemistry with biological and

00:14:38.864 --> 00:14:39.824
analytical functions.

00:14:40.844 --> 00:14:44.620
Chlorophyll contains magnesium in its coordinated pigment framework, while

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:44.824
haem

00:14:44.824 --> 00:14:47.184
groups contain iron in a porphyrin environment.

00:14:48.164 --> 00:14:50.224
Vitamin B twelve contains cobalt.

00:14:50.864 --> 00:14:54.664
These metal identities are useful starting facts, but they do not imply that

00:14:54.664 --> 00:14:58.544
every metal ion has the same biological function in every ligand environment.

00:14:59.604 --> 00:15:03.844
In qualitative analysis, complex formation can change the concentration of a

00:15:03.844 --> 00:15:07.904
free metal ion and thereby affect precipitation or dissolution equilibria.

00:15:08.924 --> 00:15:12.284
Such reasoning requires the competing equilibrium constants and solution

00:15:12.284 --> 00:15:12.804
conditions.

00:15:13.684 --> 00:15:17.904
In a standard silver complex example, two cyanide ligands each have charge

00:15:17.904 --> 00:15:18.464
minus one.

00:15:19.204 --> 00:15:23.624
A complex charge of minus one therefore places silver at oxidation state plus

00:15:23.624 --> 00:15:23.904
one.

00:15:24.764 --> 00:15:28.404
Its formation does not itself require a change in the silver oxidation state.

00:15:29.504 --> 00:15:33.254
Complex formation is also used in extraction and purification chemistry

00:15:33.254 --> 00:15:33.624
because

00:15:33.624 --> 00:15:36.524
ligands can stabilize selected dissolved metal species.

00:15:37.664 --> 00:15:41.664
Keep ligand binding, redox conversion and precipitation conceptually separate,

00:15:41.984 --> 00:15:45.644
then connect them through balanced equations when a problem supplies enough

00:15:45.644 --> 00:15:45.924
data.

00:15:46.884 --> 00:15:50.622
The purpose here is interpreting chemical roles and charge balance, rather

00:15:50.622 --> 00:15:50.824
than

00:15:50.824 --> 00:15:52.404
prescribing an experimental procedure.

00:15:52.656 --> 00:15:57.536
Coordination isomerism concerns how ligands are distributed between a complex

00:15:57.536 --> 00:15:59.416
cation and a complex anion.

00:16:00.416 --> 00:16:04.676
Compare a salt containing hexaamminecobalt three plus and hexacyanidochromate

00:16:04.676 --> 00:16:09.256
three minus with a salt containing hexaamminechromium three plus and

00:16:09.256 --> 00:16:11.976
hexacyanidocobaltate three minus.

00:16:11.976 --> 00:16:16.076
The total atom inventory is the same, but the metal to ligand connections

00:16:16.076 --> 00:16:16.436
differ.

00:16:16.436 --> 00:16:21.022
In each cation, six neutral ammonia ligands surround a metal in oxidation

00:16:21.022 --> 00:16:21.336
state

00:16:21.336 --> 00:16:21.896
plus three.

00:16:23.116 --> 00:16:27.536
In each anion, six cyanido ligands of charge minus one surround a metal in

00:16:27.536 --> 00:16:30.496
oxidation state plus three, giving overall charge minus three.

00:16:31.476 --> 00:16:34.656
Six donor atoms coordinate to each metal in this monodentate example.

00:16:35.356 --> 00:16:39.436
This exchange is different from linkage isomerism, where one ambidentate

00:16:39.436 --> 00:16:39.776
ligand

00:16:39.776 --> 00:16:40.196
changes

00:16:40.196 --> 00:16:46.416
its

00:16:46.416 --> 00:16:46.811
donor atom, and different from ionization isomerism, where coordinated and

00:16:46.811 --> 00:16:46.986
counter ions exchange roles.

00:16:46.986 --> 00:16:51.016
A formula comparison should establish the same overall composition before

00:16:51.016 --> 00:16:52.256
applying an isomer label.

00:16:53.136 --> 00:16:56.436
Merely changing an oxidation number in a written name does not create a valid

00:16:56.436 --> 00:16:56.736
pair.

00:16:57.396 --> 00:17:01.296
The example classifies connectivity; it does not imply that every conceivable

00:17:01.296 --> 00:17:04.976
exchanged salt has equal stability or can be prepared under identical

00:17:04.976 --> 00:17:05.456
conditions.

00:17:05.712 --> 00:17:10.312
Water shown in a coordination formula can be a ligand or a molecule of

00:17:10.312 --> 00:17:10.812
crystallization.

00:17:12.092 --> 00:17:16.592
Compare hexaaquachromium three chloride, with six water ligands inside the

00:17:16.592 --> 00:17:19.912
coordination sphere and three chloride counter ions, against

00:17:19.912 --> 00:17:23.312
pentaaquachloridochromium three chloride monohydrate.

00:17:23.992 --> 00:17:28.397
In the second compound, five waters and one chloride occupy six donor

00:17:28.397 --> 00:17:28.972
positions

00:17:28.972 --> 00:17:29.792
around chromium.

00:17:30.352 --> 00:17:34.552
Two chlorides remain outside and one water is outside as water of

00:17:34.552 --> 00:17:35.112
crystallization.

00:17:36.112 --> 00:17:39.753
Both have the same total inventory of chromium, three chlorines and six

00:17:39.753 --> 00:17:40.112
waters,

00:17:40.732 --> 00:17:42.192
while their coordination spheres differ.

00:17:43.112 --> 00:17:45.912
This is a hydrate or solvate isomerism comparison.

00:17:46.792 --> 00:17:50.872
In an ideal immediate chloride precipitation test that leaves the coordination

00:17:50.872 --> 00:17:55.612
sphere intact, one mole of the first compound gives three moles of silver

00:17:55.612 --> 00:17:58.612
chloride, while one mole of the second gives two.

00:17:59.082 --> 00:18:03.712
For zero point zero one zero zero mole of each, with supplied silver chloride

00:18:03.712 --> 00:18:07.892
molar mass one hundred forty three point five grams per mole, the predicted

00:18:07.892 --> 00:18:12.092
masses are four point three zero five and two point eight seven zero grams.

00:18:13.112 --> 00:18:17.292
Slow ligand substitution or other side processes can invalidate a simple outer

00:18:17.292 --> 00:18:20.392
sphere count, so the test conditions and time scale matter.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:26.680
Crystal field stabilization is measured relative to a chosen orbital energy

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:28.180
barycentre.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:32.420
For an octahedral d three ion in the elementary model, three electrons occupy

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:33.980
the lower three orbitals singly.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:38.900
Each contributes minus zero point four times the octahedral splitting, giving

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:43.280
total minus one point two times that splitting, with three unpaired electrons.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:48.960
A tetrahedral d three ion instead fills two lower orbitals singly, then places

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:53.039
the third electron in one of the upper three orbitals under the usual high

00:18:53.039 --> 00:18:53.260
spin

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:53.760
assumption.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:58.700
Lower orbitals contribute minus zero point six times the tetrahedral splitting

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:03.520
each; the upper electron contributes plus zero point four, giving net minus

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.780
zero point eight times the tetrahedral splitting.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:10.940
Both arrangements have three unpaired electrons, so equal unpaired counts do

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:13.940
not imply equal orbital stabilization or equal geometry.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:19.055
In a supplied ideal comparison, the tetrahedral splitting is four ninths of

00:19:19.055 --> 00:19:19.260
the

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:21.660
octahedral value for otherwise comparable fields.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:26.340
If octahedral splitting is eighteen thousand reciprocal centimetres,

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:30.480
tetrahedral splitting is eight thousand, and the two stabilizations are minus

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:34.200
twenty one thousand six hundred and minus six thousand four hundred reciprocal

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:35.680
centimetres.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:39.440
Actual geometry selection involves more than this one energy contribution.

00:19:39.744 --> 00:19:43.104
Ligand count and coordination number are different quantities.

00:19:44.064 --> 00:19:49.164
In a simple metal complex with one fully bound hexadentate EDTA ligand, the

00:19:49.164 --> 00:19:52.124
ligand uses two nitrogen and four oxygen donor atoms.

00:19:53.024 --> 00:19:55.764
One ligand can therefore account for six donor positions.

00:19:56.824 --> 00:20:01.104
Treating six donor atoms as six separate EDTA molecules would give the wrong

00:20:01.104 --> 00:20:02.844
composition and titration ratio.

00:20:04.344 --> 00:20:08.504
Consider a supplied one to one metal binding model at a suitable buffered p H,

00:20:08.504 --> 00:20:10.484
with no interfering ions.

00:20:11.844 --> 00:20:15.984
A twenty five point zero millilitre metal aliquot consumes twenty point zero

00:20:15.984 --> 00:20:19.784
millilitres of zero point zero one zero zero molar EDTA.

00:20:21.064 --> 00:20:25.524
Titrant amount is zero point zero zero zero two zero zero mole, equal to metal

00:20:25.524 --> 00:20:26.704
amount in the stated model.

00:20:27.744 --> 00:20:32.204
Dividing by zero point zero two five zero litre gives metal concentration zero

00:20:32.204 --> 00:20:34.224
point zero zero eight zero zero molar.

00:20:35.444 --> 00:20:40.124
EDTA protonation and metal hydrolysis depend on p H, so its effective binding

00:20:40.124 --> 00:20:44.784
strength is conditional; a large formal formation constant alone does not

00:20:44.784 --> 00:20:46.964
guarantee quantitative titration in every solution.

00:20:48.004 --> 00:20:51.824
The calculation assumes suitable reaction completion and endpoint detection.

00:20:52.884 --> 00:20:56.824
It illustrates ligand denticity and analytical stoichiometry, not an

00:20:56.824 --> 00:20:59.564
instruction to conduct a titration without a laboratory protocol.

00:20:59.856 --> 00:21:04.176
A crystal field contribution alone is not the total cost of changing spin

00:21:04.176 --> 00:21:04.476
state.

00:21:05.336 --> 00:21:09.996
For an octahedral d five ion in the elementary model, high spin places three

00:21:09.996 --> 00:21:13.876
electrons in the lower set and two in the upper set, all unpaired.

00:21:14.576 --> 00:21:18.836
Its crystal field contribution is zero, and it has no electron pairs.

00:21:19.736 --> 00:21:24.416
Low spin places all five electrons in the lower set, giving two pairs and one

00:21:24.416 --> 00:21:25.116
unpaired electron.

00:21:25.116 --> 00:21:29.716
Its crystal field contribution is minus two times the octahedral splitting.

00:21:30.776 --> 00:21:35.456
If each additional pair costs P, the low spin energy relative to high spin is

00:21:35.456 --> 00:21:38.016
minus two delta plus two P.

00:21:38.016 --> 00:21:41.636
With supplied splitting eighteen thousand reciprocal centimetres and pairing

00:21:41.636 --> 00:21:45.996
cost twenty thousand in the same units, the difference is plus four thousand.

00:21:45.996 --> 00:21:48.616
High spin is favoured by this limited comparison.

00:21:49.316 --> 00:21:53.196
The low spin field term is negative, yet its extra pairing cost is larger.

00:21:53.716 --> 00:21:57.983
Spin only moments are approximately five point nine two and one point seven

00:21:57.983 --> 00:21:59.676
three Bohr magnetons respectively.

00:22:00.716 --> 00:22:04.796
Real spin energetics require more detailed interactions than this simplified

00:22:04.796 --> 00:22:05.176
accounting.

00:22:05.616 --> 00:22:09.936
An absorption wavelength can estimate a level separation when the transition

00:22:09.936 --> 00:22:11.076
assignment is supplied.

00:22:11.676 --> 00:22:16.936
In a simplified octahedral d one example, suppose the band maximum at six

00:22:16.936 --> 00:22:20.416
hundred nanometres represents the lower to upper d level splitting.

00:22:21.616 --> 00:22:26.876
Use the supplied molar constant N A h c equal to zero point one one nine six

00:22:26.876 --> 00:22:28.636
two six joule metre per mole.

00:22:29.276 --> 00:22:33.336
Dividing by six hundred times ten to the minus nine metre gives about one

00:22:33.336 --> 00:22:35.936
hundred ninety nine point four kilojoules per mole.

00:22:36.616 --> 00:22:40.816
A second assigned band at five hundred nanometres corresponds to about two

00:22:40.816 --> 00:22:44.396
hundred thirty nine point three kilojoules per mole, a larger splitting.

00:22:45.516 --> 00:22:47.836
Shorter wavelength means higher photon energy.

00:22:48.576 --> 00:22:52.656
Do not confuse energy per photon with energy per mole of absorbed photons.

00:22:53.496 --> 00:22:57.976
Nor does an absorption maximum directly name the observed solution colour: the

00:22:57.976 --> 00:23:02.396
transmitted or reflected spectrum depends on which wavelengths are removed and

00:23:02.396 --> 00:23:03.496
on the full band shape.

00:23:04.436 --> 00:23:09.016
Other complexes may have multiple d transitions or charge transfer bands, so

00:23:09.016 --> 00:23:13.136
the assignment of a measured maximum to one splitting must be justified rather

00:23:13.136 --> 00:23:14.176
than assumed universally.

00:23:14.568 --> 00:23:18.928
A larger formation constant does not alone fix which complex dominates when

00:23:18.928 --> 00:23:20.488
free ligand concentrations differ.

00:23:21.448 --> 00:23:25.668
Consider a supplied dilute solution model with one metal ion and two competing

00:23:25.668 --> 00:23:28.188
neutral monodentate ligands, L and X.

00:23:28.848 --> 00:23:33.718
The complexes are M L two and M X two, with overall formation constants one

00:23:33.718 --> 00:23:37.668
hundred million and one million respectively, using consistent concentration

00:23:37.668 --> 00:23:38.228
conventions.

00:23:38.868 --> 00:23:44.108
Free ligand concentrations are zero point zero zero one zero zero molar L and

00:23:44.108 --> 00:23:46.408
zero point zero one zero zero molar X.

00:23:47.408 --> 00:23:50.288
Dividing the two formation expressions cancels the same free metal

00:23:50.288 --> 00:23:50.848
concentration.

00:23:51.828 --> 00:23:55.688
The complex ratio is one hundred times the square of one tenth, which equals

00:23:55.688 --> 00:23:56.008
one.

00:23:56.688 --> 00:24:00.288
Thus the two complex concentrations are equal under these specified free

00:24:00.288 --> 00:24:00.588
ligand

00:24:00.588 --> 00:24:01.088
conditions.

00:24:01.648 --> 00:24:05.968
The larger L formation constant is exactly offset by its lower free

00:24:05.968 --> 00:24:07.328
concentration in this example.

00:24:08.308 --> 00:24:11.608
Total ligand added is not automatically equal to free ligand concentration,

00:24:12.208 --> 00:24:14.568
because binding and other equilibria can consume it.

00:24:15.028 --> 00:24:19.308
The model omits mixed ligand complexes, protonation, hydrolysis and

00:24:19.308 --> 00:24:19.648
precipitation.

00:24:20.688 --> 00:24:23.908
Full speciation requires those extra balances when they are relevant.

00:24:24.624 --> 00:24:29.905
Chelation often benefits from entropy, but its thermodynamic preference must

00:24:29.905 --> 00:24:30.044
be

00:24:30.044 --> 00:24:32.344
evaluated for a specified balanced reaction.

00:24:33.424 --> 00:24:36.924
Consider an illustrative ligand exchange with supplied standard reaction

00:24:36.924 --> 00:24:42.224
enthalpy plus six point zero zero kilojoules per mole and standard reaction

00:24:42.224 --> 00:24:45.304
entropy plus forty point zero joules per mole kelvin.

00:24:46.004 --> 00:24:50.136
At three hundred kelvin, the entropy term is twelve point zero kilojoules per

00:24:50.136 --> 00:24:50.404
mole.

00:24:51.294 --> 00:24:55.724
Standard reaction Gibbs energy is therefore minus six point zero zero

00:24:55.724 --> 00:24:58.684
kilojoules per mole, despite the positive enthalpy.

00:24:59.644 --> 00:25:03.964
With R equal to eight point three one four joules per mole kelvin, the

00:25:03.964 --> 00:25:08.194
dimensionless equilibrium constant is exponential six thousand divided by

00:25:08.194 --> 00:25:08.484
eight

00:25:08.484 --> 00:25:12.024
point three one four times three hundred, about eleven point one.

00:25:12.524 --> 00:25:16.344
This example shows how the entropy term can outweigh an enthalpy cost.

00:25:16.614 --> 00:25:20.984
It does not claim that every chelate exchange has these signs or that counting

00:25:20.984 --> 00:25:24.184
liberated molecules alone supplies a numerical entropy.

00:25:25.344 --> 00:25:28.424
Solvation, ligand structure and standard states also matter.

00:25:29.444 --> 00:25:33.104
Keep reaction stoichiometry and the stated temperature attached to each

00:25:33.104 --> 00:25:36.064
thermodynamic quantity before making a stability comparison.

00:25:36.384 --> 00:25:40.604
Count a new electron configuration rather than copying the d five or d six

00:25:40.604 --> 00:25:40.964
result.

00:25:41.724 --> 00:25:46.144
In an ideal octahedral d four model, high spin places three electrons in the

00:25:46.144 --> 00:25:48.264
lower set and one in the upper set.

00:25:48.924 --> 00:25:53.284
All four are unpaired, and its crystal field contribution is negative zero

00:25:53.284 --> 00:25:56.464
point six times the splitting, with no electron pair.

00:25:57.364 --> 00:26:01.704
Low spin places all four electrons in the lower set, making one pair and

00:26:01.704 --> 00:26:03.144
leaving two unpaired electrons.

00:26:03.144 --> 00:26:08.244
Its energy on the same reference is negative one point six times the splitting

00:26:08.604 --> 00:26:09.804
plus one pairing cost.

00:26:10.744 --> 00:26:15.284
Low spin minus high spin is therefore P minus delta, not twice that expression

00:26:15.284 --> 00:26:16.764
as in some other configurations.

00:26:17.744 --> 00:26:21.129
For supplied splitting two hundred and pairing cost one hundred fifty

00:26:21.129 --> 00:26:25.004
kilojoules per mole, low spin is lower by fifty kilojoules per mole.

00:26:25.704 --> 00:26:30.304
The spin only moments are approximately four point nine zero for high spin and

00:26:30.304 --> 00:26:32.524
two point eight three Bohr magnetons for low spin.

00:26:33.444 --> 00:26:37.924
These are elementary occupancy and energy comparisons; distortions and more

00:26:37.924 --> 00:26:41.704
detailed electronic interactions are outside this supplied ideal model.

00:26:41.832 --> 00:26:45.012
A bridging ligand attaches to more than one central atom.

00:26:45.712 --> 00:26:50.192
The Greek prefix mu identifies that role; it is not the same as a ligand using

00:26:50.192 --> 00:26:52.812
two different donor atoms to chelate one metal.

00:26:53.852 --> 00:26:57.964
Consider a supplied two metal connectivity model with two terminal chlorides

00:26:57.964 --> 00:26:58.072
on

00:26:58.072 --> 00:27:01.952
each metal and two additional chloride ligands bridging both metals.

00:27:02.712 --> 00:27:06.552
There are four terminal plus two bridging chlorides, six unique chloride

00:27:06.552 --> 00:27:07.312
ligands in total.

00:27:07.892 --> 00:27:11.992
Each metal touches its two terminal chlorides and both bridges, giving

00:27:11.992 --> 00:27:14.892
coordination number four at each centre.

00:27:14.892 --> 00:27:18.272
The structure therefore has eight metal chloride contacts even though it

00:27:18.272 --> 00:27:19.712
contains only six chloride atoms.

00:27:20.552 --> 00:27:25.012
If both metals have oxidation state plus two, the complete six chloride entity

00:27:25.012 --> 00:27:27.052
has total charge negative two.

00:27:27.572 --> 00:27:31.852
This is bookkeeping for the stated connectivity, not a claim that every

00:27:31.852 --> 00:27:33.912
dissolved metal chloride adopts this dimer.

00:27:34.412 --> 00:27:38.772
The molecular formula alone cannot establish bridge positions or a unique

00:27:38.772 --> 00:27:39.252
geometry.

00:27:40.352 --> 00:27:44.426
Distinguish unique ligand count, donor contacts at one metal and total

00:27:44.426 --> 00:27:44.892
contacts

00:27:44.892 --> 00:27:47.672
in the entity before calculating coordination number.

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:52.740
Geometrical isomerism and optical isomerism are separate questions for a

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:53.420
chelate complex.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:58.860
Take the ideal octahedral cobalt complex with two neutral ethane one two

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:03.140
diamine ligands and two chlorides, carrying total charge plus one.

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:08.442
The two bidentate ligands contribute four nitrogen donors and the chlorides

00:28:08.442 --> 00:28:08.620
two

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:13.400
more, so coordination number is six and cobalt oxidation state is plus three.

00:28:14.200 --> 00:28:19.100
Adjacent chloride positions define cis, while opposite positions define trans.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:23.040
When the chelate connections are included, the cis form has two

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:25.160
nonsuperimposable mirror configurations.

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:30.000
The ideal trans form is achiral in the usual introductory treatment.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:35.020
Thus the standard count is two geometrical classes but three configurational

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:38.180
stereoisomers when the cis mirror pair is counted separately.

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:43.162
This count ignores individual chelate ring conformations and assumes the

00:28:43.162 --> 00:28:43.500
stated

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:44.440
ligand connectivity.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:49.540
A racemic cis sample can show zero net rotation even though its component

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:50.680
molecules are chiral.

00:28:51.620 --> 00:28:55.940
Do not conclude that every cis complex is optically active or that drawing two

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:59.800
mirror images automatically proves they cannot be superimposed by a proper

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:00.220
rotation.

00:29:01.296 --> 00:29:05.276
A formation constant and a ligand exchange rate measure different things.

00:29:06.516 --> 00:29:10.396
Compare two supplied one to one complex formation models under matching

00:29:10.396 --> 00:29:11.776
standard state conventions.

00:29:12.896 --> 00:29:17.602
Complex A has formation constant ten to the twelve, while B has ten to the

00:29:17.602 --> 00:29:17.856
six.

00:29:18.596 --> 00:29:22.716
A is more strongly favoured relative to its specified separated metal and

00:29:22.716 --> 00:29:23.656
ligand reference states.

00:29:24.356 --> 00:29:29.116
Now a separately defined labelled ligand exchange experiment gives first order

00:29:29.116 --> 00:29:34.396
constants zero point one zero zero per minute for A and zero point zero zero

00:29:34.396 --> 00:29:36.656
one zero zero per minute for B.

00:29:37.776 --> 00:29:41.385
Their exchange half times are natural logarithm two divided by those

00:29:41.385 --> 00:29:41.916
constants,

00:29:42.656 --> 00:29:45.876
about six point nine three and six hundred ninety three minutes.

00:29:46.856 --> 00:29:51.076
A therefore exchanges faster in this experiment despite its larger formation

00:29:51.076 --> 00:29:51.576
constant.

00:29:52.236 --> 00:29:56.176
The exchange measurement follows replacement of a labelled ligand, not

00:29:56.176 --> 00:29:59.116
necessarily loss of the total amount of complex in solution.

00:30:00.216 --> 00:30:04.649
Labile and inert describe rates under specified conditions; stable and

00:30:04.649 --> 00:30:05.156
unstable

00:30:05.156 --> 00:30:08.096
require an equilibrium reaction and reference states.

00:30:09.116 --> 00:30:12.896
Do not infer any universal numerical time boundary from this illustrative

00:30:12.896 --> 00:30:17.576
comparison, or assume that increasing a formation constant must proportionally

00:30:17.576 --> 00:30:19.156
slow every substitution pathway.

00:30:19.752 --> 00:30:23.852
Mixed ligand names require two separate decisions: charge accounting and

00:30:23.852 --> 00:30:25.052
alphabetical ligand order.

00:30:25.792 --> 00:30:29.572
Consider a salt written as a chromium complex containing four ammonia ligands,

00:30:30.112 --> 00:30:34.532
one water and one coordinated chloride, with two chloride counterions outside

00:30:34.532 --> 00:30:35.052
the brackets.

00:30:35.752 --> 00:30:37.392
The complex has charge plus two.

00:30:38.332 --> 00:30:41.852
Ammonia and water are neutral, while the bound chloride contributes minus one,

00:30:42.232 --> 00:30:43.552
so chromium is plus three.

00:30:44.132 --> 00:30:47.032
There are six donor atoms and coordination number six.

00:30:47.032 --> 00:30:51.892
For the additive name, use ammine with two m letters for ammonia, aqua for

00:30:51.892 --> 00:30:54.132
water, and chlorido for coordinated chloride.

00:30:54.852 --> 00:30:58.452
Alphabetize the ligand names without using the numerical prefix tetra to

00:30:58.452 --> 00:30:58.752
choose

00:30:58.752 --> 00:30:59.212
their order.

00:30:59.932 --> 00:31:04.247
The complex cation is tetraammineaquachloridochromium three, with three

00:31:04.247 --> 00:31:04.672
written

00:31:04.672 --> 00:31:07.852
as a Roman numeral, followed by chloride for the counterion.

00:31:08.912 --> 00:31:13.732
Compare potassium hexachloridoplatinate four: two potassium ions imply a

00:31:13.732 --> 00:31:17.412
complex charge minus two; six chlorides then require platinum plus four.

00:31:18.212 --> 00:31:21.612
The anionic complex uses the metal ending ate, here platinate.

00:31:22.472 --> 00:31:24.832
Oxidation state and complex charge are different quantities.

00:31:25.452 --> 00:31:29.532
This name determines composition but does not by itself specify every possible

00:31:29.532 --> 00:31:31.972
geometrical arrangement of the mixed ligand complex.

00:31:32.208 --> 00:31:36.748
A d electron count is a starting point, not a complete geometry prediction.

00:31:37.848 --> 00:31:41.161
Compare the usual introductory descriptions of tetrachloridonickelate two

00:31:41.161 --> 00:31:41.388
minus

00:31:41.388 --> 00:31:45.048
and tetracyanidonickelate two minus.

00:31:45.048 --> 00:31:49.388
In each complex, four ligands each carry charge minus one and the overall

00:31:49.388 --> 00:31:50.668
charge is minus two.

00:31:51.368 --> 00:31:54.668
Nickel is therefore plus two and has a d eight count in both cases.

00:31:55.388 --> 00:31:59.588
The chloride complex is normally described as tetrahedral with two unpaired

00:31:59.588 --> 00:32:03.988
electrons, whereas the cyanide complex is square planar with all electrons

00:32:03.988 --> 00:32:04.328
paired.

00:32:05.188 --> 00:32:09.028
Their spin only moments are consequently square root of eight, about two point

00:32:09.028 --> 00:32:11.828
eight three Bohr magnetons, and zero respectively.

00:32:12.788 --> 00:32:16.428
These are model values; measured moments can contain additional contributions.

00:32:17.468 --> 00:32:21.508
For a separate square planar platinum two complex with two ammonia and two

00:32:21.508 --> 00:32:25.408
chloride ligands, cis and trans arrangements are distinguishable because

00:32:25.408 --> 00:32:27.808
adjacent and opposite positions are inequivalent.

00:32:28.288 --> 00:32:31.988
An ideal tetrahedral complex with two ligands of each type has no

00:32:31.988 --> 00:32:32.728
corresponding

00:32:32.728 --> 00:32:36.248
cis and trans pair, since all vertex pairs are equivalent.

00:32:37.448 --> 00:32:40.317
Geometry must therefore be established before counting this kind of

00:32:40.317 --> 00:32:40.788
geometrical

00:32:40.788 --> 00:32:41.468
isomerism.

00:32:42.468 --> 00:32:46.788
Do not infer identical magnetism or identical isomer counts merely from the

00:32:46.788 --> 00:32:47.688
common d eight count.

00:32:47.928 --> 00:32:52.488
The high spin and low spin ledgers must be rebuilt for each electron count.

00:32:53.188 --> 00:32:57.888
In an ideal octahedral d seven configuration, the high spin filling has five

00:32:57.888 --> 00:33:00.868
electrons in the lower set and two in the upper set.

00:33:01.688 --> 00:33:05.608
There are two doubly occupied orbitals and three unpaired electrons.

00:33:06.388 --> 00:33:10.048
Its crystal field term is negative zero point eight times the octahedral

00:33:10.048 --> 00:33:14.508
splitting, and its pairing contribution is twice the supplied cost per pair.

00:33:15.168 --> 00:33:19.348
Low spin has six electrons in the lower set and one in the upper set.

00:33:20.228 --> 00:33:22.948
It contains three pairs and one unpaired electron.

00:33:23.768 --> 00:33:27.488
Its energy is negative one point eight times the splitting plus three pairing

00:33:27.488 --> 00:33:27.908
costs.

00:33:28.828 --> 00:33:32.448
Subtract high spin from low spin: the difference is pairing cost minus

00:33:32.448 --> 00:33:32.728
splitting.

00:33:33.628 --> 00:33:37.398
With supplied splitting two hundred twenty and pairing cost one hundred sixty

00:33:37.398 --> 00:33:41.628
kilojoules per mole, the low spin model is lower by sixty kilojoules per mole.

00:33:42.588 --> 00:33:46.588
The corresponding spin only moments are approximately three point eight seven

00:33:46.588 --> 00:33:49.348
and one point seven three Bohr magnetons.

00:33:50.288 --> 00:33:52.768
Both forms are paramagnetic in this simple count.

00:33:53.748 --> 00:33:57.528
These energies use the same spherical barycentre reference and a simplified

00:33:57.528 --> 00:34:02.408
constant pairing cost; a negative crystal field contribution alone is not the

00:34:02.408 --> 00:34:03.528
entire energy comparison.

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:08.500
A metal formation constant can be large while protonation reduces the ligand

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:10.840
fraction available in its binding form.

00:34:11.700 --> 00:34:15.940
Consider a supporting EDTA model at fixed temperature and buffered pH.

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:22.120
Define beta for metal plus fully deprotonated Y four minus forming one metal

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:23.400
EDTA complex.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:28.880
Define the uncomplexed ligand pool as the sum of all free protonation forms,

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:31.500
excluding ligand already bound to metal.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:36.720
If alpha is the fraction of that pool present as Y four minus, the conditional

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:39.780
concentration formation constant is alpha times beta.

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:44.940
Use a hypothetical beta of ten to the eight per molar and a supplied free

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:46.920
ligand pool of one tenth millimolar.

00:34:47.740 --> 00:34:51.971
At alpha equal to ten to the minus four, the conditional constant is ten to

00:34:51.971 --> 00:34:52.140
the

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:56.560
four per molar, so bound to free metal ratio is one and half the metal is

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:57.140
complexed.

00:34:57.520 --> 00:35:01.920
At alpha equal to ten to the minus two with the same pool, the ratio becomes

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:06.280
one hundred and the bound fraction one hundred over one hundred one.

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:09.980
These alpha values are supplied, not assigned to particular pH values.

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:14.760
The calculation excludes competing metals, metal hydrolysis and other

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:15.260
complexes.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:20.460
Total added EDTA cannot replace the free pool when binding substantially

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:21.120
depletes it.

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:26.021
A geometric isomer count must identify the geometry and remove drawings

00:35:26.021 --> 00:35:26.480
related

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:28.000
by whole molecule rotation.

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:33.700
Consider an ideal square planar complex with four distinct achiral monodentate

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:38.400
ligands A, B, C and D, with no additional ligand stereochemistry.

00:35:39.520 --> 00:35:40.520
Fix A at one position.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:45.520
Its opposite ligand can be B, C or D, giving three distinct trans pair

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:45.940
patterns.

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:50.589
Once the opposite pair is selected, exchanging the remaining two positions

00:35:50.589 --> 00:35:50.840
does

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:53.060
not generate another independent geometric pattern.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:57.220
The count is therefore three rather than twenty four labeled drawings.

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:02.880
Compare a square planar complex with two identical A ligands plus B and C.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:07.580
The two A ligands can be adjacent or opposite, giving two geometric isomers.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:12.020
An ideal square planar array of achiral point ligands also has its molecular

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:16.040
plane as a mirror plane, so these patterns do not independently create an

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:17.120
optical pair in this model.

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:21.964
This conclusion must not be transferred to tetrahedral geometry or ligands

00:36:21.964 --> 00:36:22.160
with

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:23.060
their own chirality.

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:27.860
Count opposite relationships, then test which redrawings preserve them.

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:32.284
A d electron count does not by itself determine the spin state of an

00:36:32.284 --> 00:36:32.920
octahedral

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:33.460
complex.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:38.980
For d six, the high spin arrangement places four electrons in the lower t two

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:39.040
g

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:41.380
set and two in the higher e g set.

00:36:42.020 --> 00:36:45.220
It has one paired orbital and four unpaired electrons.

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:50.800
The low spin arrangement places all six electrons in t two g, producing three

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:52.800
pairs and no unpaired electrons.

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:57.695
Using a consistent bookkeeping convention with pairing cost P for each

00:36:57.695 --> 00:36:58.140
electron

00:36:58.140 --> 00:37:03.240
pair, high spin energy is minus zero point four times the splitting plus P.

00:37:04.340 --> 00:37:09.400
Low spin energy is minus two point four times the splitting plus three P.

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:13.840
Subtracting gives low minus high equal to minus two splitting units plus two

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:14.280
pairing costs.

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:19.020
The simplified model therefore favors low spin when the splitting exceeds P.

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:23.080
With supplied splitting two hundred and pairing cost one hundred fifty

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:27.380
kilojoules per mole, low minus high is minus one hundred kilojoules per mole.

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:31.480
This model comparison omits other energetic and thermal terms.

00:37:32.540 --> 00:37:35.280
State the pairing reference before comparing two energy expressions.

00:37:35.712 --> 00:37:41.005
A carbonyl ligand is not described completely by a one way arrow from ligand

00:37:41.005 --> 00:37:41.632
to metal.

00:37:42.572 --> 00:37:47.389
In the usual transition metal carbonyl model, carbon monoxide donates through

00:37:47.389 --> 00:37:47.452
a

00:37:47.452 --> 00:37:51.621
carbon centered lone pair into a suitable metal orbital, making a sigma

00:37:51.621 --> 00:37:52.032
bonding

00:37:52.032 --> 00:37:52.592
contribution.

00:37:53.472 --> 00:37:57.832
Occupied metal orbitals can also donate electron density back into carbon

00:37:57.832 --> 00:38:00.272
monoxide pi star antibonding orbitals.

00:38:01.012 --> 00:38:05.012
These two contributions can reinforce the metal carbon interaction and are

00:38:05.012 --> 00:38:06.912
often called synergic bonding.

00:38:07.972 --> 00:38:12.192
Putting electron density into a carbon oxygen antibonding orbital weakens the

00:38:12.192 --> 00:38:14.632
internal carbon oxygen bond in this model.

00:38:15.512 --> 00:38:19.612
In suitable comparable transition metal carbonyl series, greater back donation

00:38:19.612 --> 00:38:23.652
is commonly associated with a lower carbon oxygen stretching frequency.

00:38:24.452 --> 00:38:28.202
This is not permission to assign every observed spectral shift from one

00:38:28.202 --> 00:38:28.572
orbital

00:38:28.572 --> 00:38:33.412
argument alone; charge, other ligands and the actual structure matter.

00:38:34.332 --> 00:38:38.172
The formal ligand charge of carbon monoxide remains zero in the ordinary

00:38:38.172 --> 00:38:41.592
oxidation state ledger even though electron density is shared.

00:38:42.692 --> 00:38:46.132
Distinguish a formal charge assignment from a claim that no bonding electron

00:38:46.132 --> 00:38:46.952
density has moved.

00:38:47.712 --> 00:38:52.452
This chapter is a selected bonding comparison, not a universal description of

00:38:52.452 --> 00:38:53.732
every metal carbon interaction.

00:38:54.024 --> 00:38:58.744
Electron counting in a coordination complex includes both the metal's formal d

00:38:58.744 --> 00:39:02.544
electrons and the ligand donated electrons in the chosen convention.

00:39:03.304 --> 00:39:07.904
In a supporting ionic counting comparison, carbon monoxide is a neutral two

00:39:07.904 --> 00:39:08.544
electron donor.

00:39:09.544 --> 00:39:14.284
Neutral chromium hexacarbonyl has chromium oxidation state zero and a formal d

00:39:14.284 --> 00:39:19.964
six count; six carbonyl ligands supply twelve more electrons, giving eighteen.

00:39:19.964 --> 00:39:24.784
Nickel tetracarbonyl similarly has nickel zero with d ten, plus eight donated

00:39:24.784 --> 00:39:28.204
electrons from four ligands, again eighteen.

00:39:28.204 --> 00:39:32.664
This total is not an eighteen electron d subshell: the ligand contribution is

00:39:32.664 --> 00:39:34.404
included in a valence bonding count.

00:39:35.184 --> 00:39:39.204
Nor does an eighteen electron rule establish every complex's geometry or

00:39:39.204 --> 00:39:39.624
stability.

00:39:40.584 --> 00:39:44.264
Tetrachloroplatinate two minus provides a counterexample to an absolute

00:39:44.264 --> 00:39:45.644
eighteen electron requirement.

00:39:46.304 --> 00:39:50.624
Four chloride ligands assign platinum plus two and formal d eight.

00:39:50.624 --> 00:39:54.684
In the same ionic counting convention, four two electron chloride donors add

00:39:54.684 --> 00:39:56.824
eight, giving sixteen.

00:39:56.824 --> 00:39:59.364
Such square planar d eight complexes can be stable.

00:40:00.184 --> 00:40:03.724
Keep the counting convention, oxidation state and coordination environment

00:40:03.724 --> 00:40:07.904
explicit before using an electron total as a useful organizing pattern.
